`karr create "x" --json` prints the usage block, "Unknown option: json" and exits 2. README.md ("Output and exit codes") promises `--json` for every board command, and create is the one call where an agent needs the answer machine-readable: the new id is what every following move/edit/handoff takes. Today the id has to be scraped out of `Created task 1: First task`.

## Repro

    git init t && cd t && karr init --name t
    karr create "First" --json      # usage + "Unknown option: json", exit 2

## What to build

- `create --json` emits the created card in the shape `karr show --json` uses (frontmatter plus body, one object) so a caller can pipe it into the next step. With `--json` nothing else goes to stdout.
- `agent-name --json` (`{"name":"..."}`) and `init --json` are the other two commands without it that a script meets. `backup`/`restore`/`destroy`/`set-refs`/`get-refs` stay out of scope: their output is a payload, not a record.
- Compose Role::Output like the other commands do rather than a bespoke printer; it already carries the JSON printers.

Exit codes unchanged (ADR 0002). Changes entry under {{$NEXT}}, tests in t/.

Exact list of commands without --json today: create, init, sync, backup, restore, destroy, set-refs, get-refs, agent-name. README (Output and exit codes) and the skill now say so instead of claiming every command takes it; this ticket closes create, init and agent-name, and both texts need the sentence adjusted when it lands.

Pointers: Role::Output carries the --json option and the printers; Cmd::Show's JSON is the card shape to emit; Cmd::Init's execute is where init reports the board name and the .gitignore entries it added or left alone, and {"board":{"name":...},"gitignore":[...]} is enough of a payload there. Plain output stays as it is without --json. Acceptance: karr create x --json | jq .id prints the new id, and the sync progress lines stay on STDERR so the JSON decodes whole.

